There are two outcomes to reach: whether the first is older or not, and whether they are exactly matching. Rather than parse into separate numbers, the strings can be split on periods and compared in triplets. Each comparison except a mismatch gives no result, and control passes on to the next component. If the string lengths differ, the shorter one is older. If every component matches, the versions are equal. Conversion to integer per component is unnecessary, because in a canonical layout of one or more decimal digits all longer strings are larger than shorter ones. The resulting ordering agrees with numeric comparison. Shortening the string to the major number and padding with zeros yields the same ranking.

The function bodies that follow make use of this comparison.

