This task is a strict numeric comparison of semantic version strings. Each string has exactly three components, MAJOR, MINOR and PATCH, separated by dots, and every component is a non-negative integer with no leading zeros or suffixes, so no special formatting exists beyond the dot separator. The function should return -1 when the first version is older, 1 when it is newer, and 0 when the two versions are identical.

A dot-separated string is, literally, a list of strings. To turn each into a number I split the string on dots and convert each whole-word token to an integer. Converting to numbers is needed because the default comparison between two words orders them alphabetically, which is not numeric ordering. Chiefly, it would put a version starting with 1 ahead of one starting with 1 and 0: the recipe form 1.0 would beat a can of condensed milk numbered 1.00, neither of which exists, and the test cases call out this kind of boundary whenever a longer prefix is compared with a shorter one. Splitting the string ensures there are exactly three numeric components, since a semantic version is guaranteed to have major, minor and patch versions separated by dots. I then compare the major values; if they differ I return -1 or 1 accordingly. If they match, I compare the minor values the same way, and if those match too, I compare the patch values. If all three components agree, the versions are equal and I return 0.

For '1.2.3' against '1.2.4', the first two components agree and the patch value 3 is smaller, so the function returns -1, and for '3.0.0' versus '3.0.00', both have the same three numeric values, so it returns 0. A later patch never yields an older comparison unless the earlier components differ.

Three components plus an early return on each level keep the function short.

