This is a document with undo and redo, so I track the current content and the list of values that were undone and should be redone.

Each "do" command appends its value to the document and empties the redo list, because the document has changed. An "undo" command removes the last value from the document and puts it in front of the redo list, unless the document is already empty, in which case it does nothing. A "redo" command takes the first entry from the redo list and puts it at the end of the document, unless the redo list is empty, in which case it also does nothing. After every command, the document is a list in order.

Therefore a two-command document can never be half redone, and nothing is lost that could come back.

I represent the document as an ordinary list, and the redo history as a separate list holding the most recently undone value first. When the commands finish, I return the document, taken destructively so neither list survives the function call.

