A clean way to do this without looking at the grid special case by case is to simulate a flood that erases every group of 1s. Starting from the top left, whenever a cell has the value 1, I mark it as visited, then look in the four directions, skipping outside the grid and skipping already visited or 0 cells. Each flood that begins at a 1 and ends at its boundary counts as one island, and the order in which I visit cells does not affect the number of floods. Small grids such as the examples pose no difficulty.

For an untested input, take a 3-row, 3-column grid holding a 1 at the top left, 1s running to the right on the middle row, and a lone 1 on the bottom right, with 0s elsewhere. Starting a flood at the top-left 1 pours over the three middle-row cells and stops at the single bottom-right cell, leaving a second flood touching only that cell, so the function returns 2, the expected count. With nothing but 0s it finds no starting cell and returns 0. The counting happens completely inside the grid and contains no reference to the example data.

